[[input:ans]] [[validation:ans]]]]&gt;&lt;/text&gt;
    &lt;/questiontext&gt;
    &lt;generalfeedback format="html"&gt;
      &lt;text&gt;As can be seen by multiplying out a general, factorised quadratic:
\[ (cx+a)(dx+b) = cdx^2+(da+cb)x+ab, \] 
\(a\) and \(b\) are factors of the constant term, and \(c\) and \(d\) are factors of the \(x^2\) coefficient so there are only a finite number of (integer) cases that are possible. With practice it should be simple enough to find \(a\), \(b\), \(c\) and \(d\).

In this example: 

\[ {@expand((cf1*v+a)*(cf2*v+b))@}. \]
Equating with the general quadratic above gives:
\[{cd = {@cf1*cf2@}}.\]
\[{da+cb = {@cf2*a+cf1*b@}}.\]
\[{ab = {@a*b@}}.\]

The correct values are: \(a = {@a@}\), and \(b = {@b@}\), \(c = {@cf1@}\) and, \(d = {@cf2@}\). By substituting these back into the general quadratic, and factorising constants out, we obtain the factored form: \[{@ta@}.\]&lt;/text&gt;
