Use columnspace_basis(A)/columnspace or rowspace_basis(A)/rowspace for the corresponding tasks. The older columns and rows options instead require a nonempty independent reference basis in PRT tans. If that basis spans the wrong space, the test will grade the wrong target. The input model is checked against the PRT at build time. Add qtests with another correct basis, an incomplete or dependent family, and a vector outside the space.
The built-in Python matrix tests accept exact rational entries (integers and fractions), not decimals or symbolic parameters. Matrix inputs have 1–16 rows or columns and at most 64 entries. Set enough learner-selectable columns or rows for the answer; matrix input explains resizing.
For MatrixProperty, set sans to the matrix input and choose one of its three options in testoptions. For determinant \(1\), use &lt;tans&gt;1&lt;/tans&gt; and &lt;testoptions&gt;determinant&lt;/testoptions&gt;; the input’s own tans holds a model matrix. Assign true/false branch scores on each PRT explicitly. The editor never supplies cell marks.


Author the SPD checker
Save this complete file as questions/spd-checker.py. It registers one function, is_spd, and checks exact fractions, symmetry and positive leading principal determinants:
"""Check symmetric positive definiteness for exact rational matrices."""


def is_spd(ansm, expected):
    if any(entry.is_Rational is not True for entry in ansm):
        return {"valid": False, "correct": False,
                "feedback": "Use integers or exact fractions in every cell."}

    if ansm.rows != ansm.cols or ansm != ansm.T:
        positive = False
    else:
        # Sylvester's criterion for real symmetric matrices.
        positive = all(ansm[:k, :k].det() &gt; 0 for k in range(1, ansm.rows + 1))

    correct = bool(positive) == bool(expected)
    return {"valid": True, "correct": correct,
            "feedback": "" if correct else
                "The matrix must be symmetric, with every leading principal determinant positive."}


CHECKERS = {"is_spd": is_spd}
The function receives the parsed learner matrix as ansm and the PRT’s expected property result as expected. A non-rational entry is invalid; a well-formed matrix that fails the property follows the false branch. A plain Python True or False can replace the dictionary when no extra feedback is needed.
The complete XML question sets the matrix input’s model answer to &lt;tans&gt;matrix([1,0],[0,1])&lt;/tans&gt;, which fixes the editor at \(2\times2\) but does not limit correct responses to the identity. Its PRT passes the expected property result and awards marks:
